# Everything below this line is skipped for a developer's working tree and NOT
# for CI. `.secretlintignore.ci` is this file truncated here, and
# `check:secrets:tracked` scans what git tracks against that, so an exemption
# added below can only ever excuse an untracked local file.
# >>> local-only, stripped from .secretlintignore.ci <<<

# The local working copy of the environment files. README.md tells a contributor
# to copy packages/database/.env.example to .env before running anything, and
# `pnpm verify` then fails on the very file it just told them to write, because
# check:secrets passes --no-gitignore and so scans it.
#
# The exemption cannot stand on its own, because secretlint honours this file in
# CI too and the rule it disarms there is the one worth having: the repository's
# hardest policy is that no environment file but .env.example belongs in git, and
# @secretlint/secretlint-rule-no-dotenv is the only scanner that enforces it by
# NAME rather than by pattern. Gitleaks and GitGuardian look for shapes, so a
# deployment credential with no recognisable shape walks straight past them. The
# tracked scan above is what pays for this.
.env
packages/database/.env
